the state is one selection, two ends. its shape is read off them and
never stored, the way x reads it to decide whether to grow —
`ed/5-editor/13-whole.go:/a.col == 0/`:

	caret   the ends meet
	lines   anchor at a line start, head on a line break
	span    any other range

every key is a transition from one shape to the next:

	point   h l 0 $ g G :                 a caret at the target
	carry   j k pgup pgdn                 the same, but lines move whole
	aim     w b e m { } f t F T           a span, head to target
	find    / n N *                       the match
	shape   x %                           lines, or all; x grows lines
	verb    the act and insert keys       by what became of the text
	clear   space                         a caret
	extend  v                             point, carry, aim, find move
	                                      the head

a change is a noun and a verb. vim types the two as one command — dw —
so its . repeats both. ed selects first, so they come apart, and each
half wants a memory of its own:

	same verb, new noun    pick the noun, then .        n .
	same noun, new verb    the verb leaves it selected  xx > y

. is the first half, `ed/6-commands/64-repeat.go`. its change is the
keys that took a snapshot, and only an edit takes one: R keeps the text
it replaced without one, so . never repeats a read —
`ed/1-buffer/09-reset.go:/takes no snapshot/`. the second half is the
verb row, by what became of the text it was given:

	removed    d D X J        a caret where it was
	inserted   i a A o O c C  a caret on its last rune, so p p p
	           p P            appends and typing runs on
	kept       y r > <        the selection, on the same text

u always kept it, handing back the range the edit took —
`ed/5-editor/09-recall.go:/ready to retry/`. r > < dropped it for a
caret at their start until `ed/5-editor/12-follow.go`, and on two lines
a b that cost:

	xx > .    \t\ta \tb      line 1 twice, line 2 once
	xx > >    \t\ta \tb      the same, and no . in it
	xx 3>     \t\t\ta \tb    a count replays >, so counts broke
	                         here before . existed

it is cur's rule one level down: rm, making nothing, retreats to the
parent, as d leaves a caret where the text was —
`todo/idea:/cur follows what the verb made/`. they part at inserts: cp
moves cur to the copy, ed leaves a caret after what it put in.
rejected, the insert selected: p over its own paste would replace it,
and y p p p would be one paste.

carry

point is the row that drops a shape: x l trades the line for a caret.
carry is the row that keeps one: on lines, j k and the page keys move
the lines. it is a row, not a mode — nothing is stored, so nothing is
left to unset — and with v on, j and k still grow. the choice is made
in one place, `ed/6-commands/01-movehead.go:/whole/`.

a carried block slides, one line a j: 1-4 becomes 2-5. a move keeps
its own distance, a line for j and a page for pgdn, and the shape
decides only whether the selection survives it. rejected, step: 1-4 to
5-8, so j . walks blocks as n . walks matches. it ties j's distance to
the block's height, and only slide can spell the other — 4j on four
lines is a step, but nothing slides once j steps. at either edge of
the buffer the block goes as far as it can, its height kept.

reading the shape off the ends costs two things. a hop that ends on
whole lines is lines: w on a one-word line takes the break, so j
carries what it used to drop, as x already read it. and a blank line
holds one state where there are two: its break is all the line is, so
a caret there is the line. j moves it as a caret, since a caret walks
through blank lines all the time, so a single line carried onto one
lands as that caret and the next j walks it on. a stored shape would
tell the two apart, and a stored shape is the mode carry is not.

p replaces a selection, so x y j p now copies a line over the next one
where it used to paste below it; x y j space p is the old meaning.

